#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:9
msgid "Creating a simple ODE model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:11
msgid ""
"In this series of notebooks, we will run through the steps involved in "
"creating a new model within pybamm. Before using pybamm we recommend "
"following the `Getting Started <../getting_started/tutorial-1-how-to-run-a-"
"model.ipynb>`__ guides."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:13
msgid "In this notebook we create and solve the following simple ODE model:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:15
msgid "\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d} t} = 4x - 2y, \\quad x(0) = 1,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:20
msgid "\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} t} = 3x - y, \\quad y(0) = 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:25
msgid ""
"We begin by importing the PyBaMM library into this notebook, along with "
"NumPy and Matplotlib, which we use for plotting:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[1]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"import numpy as np\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:50
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:56
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:63
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:75
msgid "Setting up the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:86
msgid ""
"We first initialise the model using the ``BaseModel`` class. This sets up "
"the required structure for our model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model = pybamm.BaseModel()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:107
msgid ""
"Next, we define the variables in the model using the ``Variable`` class. In "
"more complicated models we can give the variables more informative string "
"names, but here we simply name the variables \"x\" and \"y\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"x = pybamm.Variable(\"x\")\n"
"y = pybamm.Variable(\"y\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:129
msgid ""
"We can now use the symbols we have created for our variables to write out "
"our governing equations. Note that the governing equations must be provied "
"in the explicit form ``d/dt = rhs`` since pybamm only stores the right hand "
"side (rhs) and assumes that the left hand side is the time derivative."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"dxdt = 4 * x - 2 * y\n"
"dydt = 3 * x - y"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:151
msgid ""
"The governing equations must then be added to the dictionary ``model.rhs``. "
"The dictionary stores key and item pairs, where the key is the variable "
"which is governed by the equation stored in the corresponding item. Note "
"that the keys are the symbols that represent the variables and are not the "
"variable names (e.g. the key is ``x``, not the string \"x\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.rhs = {x: dxdt, y: dydt}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:172
msgid ""
"The initial conditions are also stored in a dictionary, ``model."
"initial_conditions``, which again uses the variable as the key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.initial_conditions = {x: pybamm.Scalar(1), y: pybamm.Scalar(2)}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:193
msgid ""
"Finally, we can add any variables of interest to our model. Note that these "
"can be things other than the variables that are solved for. For example, we "
"may want to store the variable defined by :math:`z=x+4y` as a model output. "
"Variables are added to the model using the ``model.variables`` dictionary as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.variables = {\"x\": x, \"y\": y, \"z\": x + 4 * y}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:214
msgid ""
"Note that the keys of this dictionary are strings (i.e. the names of the "
"variables). The string names can be different from the variable symbol, and "
"should in general be something informative. The model is now completely "
"defined and is ready to be discretised and solved!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:226
msgid "Using the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:237
msgid ""
"We first discretise the model using the ``pybamm.Discretisation`` class. "
"Calling the method ``process_model`` turns the model variables into a "
"``pybamm.StateVector`` object that can be passed to a solver. Since the "
"model is a system of ODEs we do not need to provide a mesh or worry about "
"any spatial dependence, so we can use the default discretisation. Details on "
"how to provide a mesh will be covered in the following notebook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation()  # use the default discretisation\n"
"disc.process_model(model);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:259
msgid ""
"Now that the model has been discretised it is ready to be solved. Here we "
"choose the ODE solver ``pybamm.ScipySolver`` and solve, returning the "
"solution at 20 time points in the interval :math:`t \\in [0, 1]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solver = pybamm.ScipySolver()\n"
"t = np.linspace(0, 1, 20)\n"
"solution = solver.solve(model, t)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:282
msgid ""
"After solving, we can extract the variables from the solution. These are "
"automatically post-processed so that the solutions can be called at any "
"time :math:`t` using interpolation. The times at which the model was solved "
"at are stored in ``solution.t`` and the statevectors at those times are "
"stored in ``solution.y``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[10]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"t_sol, y_sol = solution.t, solution.y  # get solution times and states\n"
"x = solution[\"x\"]  # extract and process x from the solution\n"
"y = solution[\"y\"]  # extract and process y from the solution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:305
msgid "We then plot the numerical solution against the exact solution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[11]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/1-an-ode-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"t_fine = np.linspace(0, t[-1], 1000)\n"
"\n"
"fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4))\n"
"ax1.plot(t_fine, 2 * np.exp(t_fine) - np.exp(2 * t_fine), t_sol, x(t_sol), "
"\"o\")\n"
"ax1.set_xlabel(\"t\")\n"
"ax1.legend([\"2*exp(t) - exp(2*t)\", \"x\"], loc=\"best\")\n"
"\n"
"ax2.plot(t_fine, 3 * np.exp(t_fine) - np.exp(2 * t_fine), t_sol, y(t_sol), "
"\"o\")\n"
"ax2.set_xlabel(\"t\")\n"
"ax2.legend([\"3*exp(t) - exp(2*t)\", \"y\"], loc=\"best\")\n"
"\n"
"plt.tight_layout()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

